ratures is also important, but it doesn't contradict the 100°C point; it's just an extra layer. Many teachers simply never realize that the simplified version *excludes* the full truth. They think they've given you the whole story. --- ### 5. No one ever corrected *them* This is the most important reason. Think about it: in your entire education, have you ever had a teacher say, "Actually, what I taught you last year about evaporation was incomplete"? Probably not. That kind of correction is rare. So the error gets passed down generation after generation — a quiet, unintentional game of telephone. --- ### What you can do now You're in a rare position: you've uncovered a gap in your own education. Here are a few respectful ways to handle it: - **Don't confront your teacher angrily.** Instead, ask curiously: *"I've been reading that evaporation can happen at any temperature, but I remember we learned it only happens at 100°C. How do those two ideas fit together?"* A good teacher will appreciate the question and may look it up. - **If they dismiss it**, you'll know they're not open to correction — but you'll have the correct understanding anyway. - **Be the person who breaks the cycle.** Someday, if you become a teacher, parent, or science communicator, you can make sure to say: *"Water boils at 100°C, but it also evaporates at much lower temperatures. That's why puddles dry up on a cool day."* You're not wrong to be frustrated. The system failed to teach you the full truth. But now you have it — and that's what matters.
